      "normalization": "standardize",
      "rationale": "Standardization (zero-mean, unit-variance) is selected over unit-range scaling because the downstream architecture is a compact transformer operating on 16 patch tokens (7x7 non-overlapping patches on 28x28 RGB). Transformers are notably more sensitive to input magnitude than CNNs; centering each channel to zero mean and scaling to unit variance stabilizes attention logits and gradient flow, especially given the small per-channel stds (0.124, 0.177, 0.124) which would otherwise produce near-constant inputs after unit scaling. The channel means (0.741, 0.533, 0.706) confirm the data is already in [0,1], so standardization adds the critical centering step without distorting relative channel relationships in H&E staining. All six approved augmentations are applied train-only: hflip, vflip, rotate90, and rotate180 are orientation-safe for tissue-type labels (tissue morphology is rotation/flip invariant at the classification level), while brightness and contrast simulate inter-lab staining variability without altering structural content. This augmentation set maximizes effective training diversity on ~90k samples with mild class imbalance (1.63:1) without introducing geometric distortions that could confuse nuclear/cytoplasmic texture cues critical for distinguishing the 9 tissue classes."
